// `s[from:to]`, in the two meanings that were being spelled the same way.
//
// Nine packages carried a private `u8[] slice(u8[], i32, i32)` and they had already drifted into three
// answers for a range that is not valid (wac-mono 0093):
//
//   - `ens`, `tor/microdesc`, `tor/relayd` allocated `to - from` and read — so a bad range **traps**,
//     and a backwards one traps on a negative length;
//   - `tls/x509` and `tor/hsdesc` checked the range and **returned empty**;
//   - `ssz/container`, `http/incoming`, `http/request`, `server/routes` clamped `to` up to `from` and
//     then read — so a backwards range is empty and an out-of-range one still traps.
//
// The same call with the same arguments therefore did three different things depending on which file it
// was written in, and nothing said so. That is worse than any one of the three: a reader cannot tell
// from the call site which they are getting, and neither can the next person to move code between
// packages.
//
// **Two functions, named for what they do**, which is the third option the issue argued for:
//
//   - `slice` requires the range to be valid and traps otherwise. It is for a caller that has *already*
//     checked — `ssz`'s `rootAt` refuses a bad span before it slices, and `http`'s body reads compare
//     `input.len() - at` against the length first. There the trap is an assertion, and if it ever fires
//     the bug is in the check above it.
//   - `clamped` takes any two integers and answers with whatever part of them is inside the array. It is
//     for a caller that means "as much of this as exists", and a parser reading attacker-supplied bytes
//     that would rather refuse than abort the process.
//
// What neither does is guess. The old naive copies trapped *by accident* — an out-of-bounds read rather
// than a stated precondition — so a caller could not tell a deliberate limit from an overlooked one.

/**
 * `s[from:to]`, copied out. The range must satisfy `0 <= from <= to <= s.len()`.
 *
 * Traps otherwise, deliberately and before the read: a slice past the end is a bug at the call site, and
 * this repo's stance is that a gap fails rather than being approximated. Use `clamped` where a bad range
 * is *input* rather than a mistake — the point of two names is that the call site says which it means.
 */
export u8[] slice(u8[] s, i32 from, i32 to) {
  if (from < 0 || to < from || to > s.len()) { trap; }
  u8[] out = u8[to - from]();
  out.copyFrom(s, from, 0, to - from);
  return out;
}

/**
 * `s[from:to]`, clamped into the array: whatever part of that range exists, and empty when none does.
 *
 * Never traps and never returns a short array without meaning to — `clamped(s, -5, 2)` is `s[0:2]` and
 * `clamped(s, 4, 1)` is empty, because a range that runs backwards contains nothing. A caller that
 * cannot tell "nothing there" from "your arguments were wrong" wants `slice` and a check of its own.
 */
export u8[] clamped(u8[] s, i32 from, i32 to) {
  i32 lo = from < 0 ? 0 : (from > s.len() ? s.len() : from);
  i32 hi = to < 0 ? 0 : (to > s.len() ? s.len() : to);
  if (hi < lo) { hi = lo; }
  u8[] out = u8[hi - lo]();
  out.copyFrom(s, lo, 0, hi - lo);
  return out;
}

/**
 * Whether two byte arrays hold the same bytes.
 *
 * Thirty-eight files had written this out — `same` in twenty-four of them and `sameBytes` in sixteen —
 * and two had drifted (wac-mono 0093 is the same story about `slice`):
 *
 *   - `tor/consensusgen`'s dropped the length check, so it read past the end of the shorter array or
 *     answered *true* for a prefix, depending on which was shorter. Its one caller compares the lengths
 *     first, so nothing was wrong yet; the next caller would have inherited the trap.
 *   - `tor/dirstep`'s answered **false for two empty arrays**, which is not equality. Every key it
 *     compares is 32 bytes and `filed` refuses any other length, so that clause was dead — but it is the
 *     kind of dead clause that is copied into somewhere it is not.
 *
 * Empty equals empty here. A caller that means "present and equal" should say `a.len() > 0 && equal(...)`
 * at the call site, where the reader can see it.
 */
export bool equal(u8[] a, u8[] b) {
  if (a.len() != b.len()) { return false; }
  for (i32 i = 0; i < a.len(); i++) {
    if (a[i] != b[i]) { return false; }
  }
  return true;
}
